0 @N6078@ NOTE https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleanor_of_Aragon,_Queen_of_Cyprus
0 @N6079@ NOTE https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleanor_of_Aragon,_Queen_of_Cyprus
0 @N6080@ NOTE === Resumen de la vida de la persona ===
1 CONT Eleanor of Aragon (1333 – 26 December 1417) was Queen consort of Cyprus by marriage to Peter I of Cyprus.[1] She was regent of Cyprus during the absence of her spouse in 1366, and regent during the minority of her son Peter II of Cyprus from 136
1 CONC 9.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Eleanor was a member of the House of Barcelona as the daughter of Peter of Aragon and Joan of Foix.
1 CONT 
1 CONT To enhance his kingdom's political and economic power in the Mediterranean, King Peter IV of Aragon arranged a marriage in 1353 between his cousin Eleanor and Peter I of Cyprus. By this marriage Eleanor became Queen of Cyprus and titular Queen of J
1 CONC erusalem and Armenia.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The reign of Eleanor's husband was not a windfall for Cyprus. Her husband kept mistresses, which she was unable to endure. In 1366, when Peter went off on a crusade against Alexandria, he made Eleanor regent of Cyprus.
1 CONT 
1 CONT After the return of Peter I from a long trip to Europe, Eleanor was accused of having committed adultery with John of Morf, the titular of Edessa. The court convened, but subsequently dismissed the charges. Peter then bullied Eleanor's favored nobl
1 CONC es, including his own brothers. On 17 January 1369, Peter was eventually murdered in the arms of a lover, perhaps on the orders of his brothers John and James.
1 CONT 
1 CONT On behalf of her son, Eleanor was again regent, together with the brothers of the king, John and James. She stood first in Cyprus to prevent the throne of her son from being usurped.
1 CONT In a secret message, she invited the Genoese to a de facto invasion of Cyprus, to take revenge on her brothers-in-law. With the help of the betrayal of John Morf, they were conquered by the Genoese in 1373 and captured James. Eleanor led the assass
1 CONC ination of John in 1374.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1378, Peter II married Valentina Visconti, daughter of Barnabò Visconti. The first choice of his marriage would have been to a daughter of John V Palaiologos; this suggestion was rejected for political reasons, since the Latins did not encourag
1 CONC e the marriage of Peter to a Greek princess. The justification that was given to the Palaiologos messengers was that the king was busy with the dangers that threatened Cyprus because of the Genoese invasion of the island.
1 CONT It is known that Eleanor did not get along with her daughter-in-law Valentina due to being involved in many issues and scandals.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1381, the now 44-year-old Eleanor was forced to return to Catalonia, in order to prevent more problems with Valentina.
1 CONT 
1 CONT After her return, her cousin, Peter IV of Aragon, entrusted Eleanor with the city of Valls. She was co-ruler with the archbishop of Tarragona.
1 CONT Eleanor moved into the archbishop's palace in Valls. This changed within a short time into a sovereign court with numerous officials and minions; some had accompanied her from Cyprus.
1 CONT It was necessary in Valls that a tax had to be paid on the import of wine. The favourites of the Queen, however, insisted that they were exempt from these taxes. This led to a revolt, with casualties on both sides. There was no choice but for her f
1 CONC riends to pay the taxes.
1 CONT 
1 CONT After the death of King Peter IV, power was transferred to his son and successor, King John I. The clashes between the population and the Queen Dowager of Cyprus, however, still continued. For alleged insults against the citizens, they broke into t
1 CONC he palace and killed John, brother and butler to Eleanor; he was murdered before Eleanor's eyes. Terrified, Eleanor fled to territory of her brother. After twelve years of living in Valls, Eleanor never returned.
1 CONT 
1 CONT She died on 26 December 1416 in the Castle of Falset, Catalonia.
