1 CONC a qual se li dona sepultura en lo cementiri de la Iglesia parroquial de CELRA als sis de dit mes y any qual dia se celebraren los oficis de enterro y eixida ab la assistència del prebreres de CELRA i mestre y se dona a quscum prevere una lliur
1 CONC a i quatre sous per haberi saquela y a lo mestre y a mi per dret de parroco set sous y sis y tres de extremuncio per esser de dies y als dos maig de dit any ce li celebraren las honres ab la dita assistència de preveres y mestre.
0 @N11352@ NOTE https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_I_of_Cyprus
0 @N11354@ NOTE https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_I_of_Cyprus
0 @N11355@ NOTE === Resumen de la vida de la persona ===
1 CONT Peter I (9 October 1328 – 17 January 1369) was King of Cyprus and titular King of Jerusalem from his father's abdication on 24 November 1358 until his death in 1369. He was invested as titular Count of Tripoli in 1346. As King of Cyprus, he had s
1 CONC ome military successes, but he was unable to complete many of his plans due to internal disputes that culminated in his assassination at the hands of three of his knights.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Peter was born in Nicosia in 1328, the second son of Hugh IV of Cyprus, the first by his second wife Alice of Ibelin.[1] Hugh's heir apparent was his first born son, Guy, who had married Marie of Bourbon. Guy died before his father, however; and th
1 CONC ough his son, also named Hugh, demanded the throne, Peter was crowned King of Cyprus by Guy of Ibelin, bishop of Limassol in the Cathedral of Santa Sophia, Nicosia on 24 November 1359.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1349 he traveled secretly to Europe with his brother John.[3] This upset their father who sent ships to find his sons and bring them back.[3] When they were brought back, Peter and John were imprisoned for leaving without his permission. Despit
1 CONC e Leontios Machairas stating Peter and John were held for three days, William of Machaut states that Peter was imprisoned for two months and nine days.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Peter was crowned as titular King of Jerusalem in Saint Nicholas Cathedral in Famagusta on 5 April 1360, succeeding his father.[4] Upon the fall of Acre in 1291, Cyprus became the last stronghold of Christianity in the Middle East.[5] Peter underst
1 CONC ood the importance of his kingdom, and believed that his mission was to fight Islam. He had ambitions to retake the Kingdom of Jerusalem to which the house of Lusignan yet pretended.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Soon after 28 June 1342 he married Eschiva de Montfort (d. before 1350),[6] only daughter and heiress of Humphrey de Montfort (1305 – 24 June 1326), Constable of Cyprus and titular Lord of Toron, and Eschive d'Ibelin. Eschiva died before 1350 whi
1 CONC le Peter was still a teenager and the marriage was childless.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1353 he married Eleanor of Aragon-Gandia (1333 – 26 December 1416 in Barcelona and buried there),[7] daughter of Pedro, Infante of Aragon, Conde de Ribagorza, Ampurias y Prades, Seneschal of Catalonia, and Jeanne de Foix (died before Novembe
1 CONC r 1358). Eleanor was crowned Queen of Cyprus on 24 November 1358 and Queen of Jerusalem on 5 April 1360.
1 CONT 
1 CONT His passion for his second wife was much remarked upon by chroniclers; the chronicle of Machairas tells how Peter always slept with Eleanora's night-dress in his arms:
1 CONT "[S]o for the love which [King Peter] had promised that, wherever he was, he would take [Queen Eleanora's] shift to lay it at night in his arms when he slept, and he made his chamberlain always bring with him the queen's shift, and had him put it i
1 CONC n his bed."
1 CONT 
1 CONT Peter and Eleanor had:
1 CONT - Peter II of Lusignan (c. 1357–1382), succeeded him as King of Cyprus and Jerusalem
1 CONT - Mariette of Lusignan (c. 1360 – c. 1397),[1] once engaged to Carlo Visconti and married in 1385 to her cousin James/Jacques de Lusignan (d. 1395/1397), titular Count of Tripoli, son of John of Lusignan and wife Alix d'Ibelin, and had issue
1 CONT - Eschiva of Lusignan (d. before 1369), died young
1 CONT 
1 CONT As Peter II of Cyprus remained heirless, the crown of Cyprus was passed to his uncle James I of Cyprus, son of Hugh IV of Cyprus and Alix of Ibelin.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ————————————
1 CONT 
1 CONT Peter I, King of Cyprus was the son of Hugh IV, King of Cyprus.1 He died in 1369.1
1 CONT      He held the office of Co-regent of Cyprus from 1358 to 1359.1 He succeeded as the King Peter I of Cyprus in 1359.1
1 CONT Child of Peter I, King of Cyprus
1 CONT Peter II, King of Cyprus+1 d. 1382
1 CONT Citations
1 CONT [S38] John Morby, Dynasties of the World: a chronological and genealogical handbook (Oxford, Oxfordshire, U.K.: Oxford University Press, 1989), page 173. Hereinafter cited as Dynasties of the World.
